WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Welcome to our training on how to set one-day default handling time.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.320
In this video, we'll show you how to improve your customer delivery promise

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.880
by changing your default handling time from two days to one day.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.143
Why is handling time so important?

00:00:15.143 --> 00:00:18.160
It's a key part of earning customer trust.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.800
Every offer in the Amazon store comes with a customer delivery promise.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.280
This is the range of dates in which a customer can expect an order to arrive.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.160
The customer delivery promise considers handling time,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.480
which is the number of days needed for a seller to prepare and ship orders;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.280
transit time, which is the number of days your selected carrier needs to deliver packages;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.760
and occasionally, additional buffers to protect your delivery promise

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.200
in case of carrier delivery issues or weather events.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.720
It's important that customers receive their orders before the final date of the customer delivery

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.040
promise.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.600
Products that arrive late make customers doubt if you can deliver on time.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.280
Late products also hurt your account health.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.680
If a customer sees two offers in the Amazon store that are the same price

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.320
but have different customer delivery promises,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.600
they're more likely to choose the offer with the shortest delivery time.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.400
Thus, it's in your best interest to have the most accurate handling time possible.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.393
A 2019 study found that merchant fulfilled sellers in the U.

00:01:21.393 --> 00:01:21.760
S.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.920
overestimate the number of days they need for handling time for domestic orders

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.080
by an average of 53 percent.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.960
That means that their customer delivery promises are longer than they need to be,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.280
which makes for missed opportunities.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.320
When you created your seller account, your default handling time was set to two business days.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.960
It might be helpful, however, to consider whether or not your orders need that long.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.360
If you can prepare orders more quickly, shortening your handling time will make your

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.417
offers more appealing to customers.

00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:55.120
Consider switching to a one-day default handling time.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.840
Note that one day does not mean same day.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.560
A one-day handling time means that an order will be prepared and shipped

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.320
by the end of the next business day.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.640
You can change handling times on a per-ASIN basis,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.200
but it's a good idea to change your default handling time

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.680
if you can prepare most of your orders in one day.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.720
To set your default handling time, go to the seller central home page

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.880
and open the settings menu by selecting the gear icon in the upper right.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.000
Select shipping settings and then choose general shipping settings on the next screen.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.040
Scroll down until you find handling time.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.480
If you haven't changed anything since creating your account,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.080
this will be set to two days.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.240
Select edit to the right, select the circle in front of one day

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.680
under choose your default handling time, then select save.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.120
It may take up to 30 minutes for the new handling time to be reflected in your delivery promise.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.880
Because setting a new default handling time means adjusting

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.160
the customer delivery promise for every one of your ASINs,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.560
we only allow you to change your handling time once every 24 hours.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.800
If you feel that you can process most orders within one day,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.920
but are worried about having more orders than you can handle,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.360
or products in your catalog that require special attention,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.507
we can help.

00:03:12.507 --> 00:03:14.800
You can use order handling capacity

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.000
to set limits on how many orders you need to fulfill in the day.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.080
If you have some products that take longer to package than others,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.080
you can also set an ASIN specific handling time for those ASINs.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.240
You can find more information about setting order handling capacity

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.720
and ASIN specific handling time in other videos from this series,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.720
or by searching for those terms on the Amazon seller central home page.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.880
Remember, having an accurate customer delivery promise

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.920
is an important part of being a successful seller.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.120
Customers prefer offers with shorter delivery times,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.920
so if you can process orders in a single day,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.840
setting a one-day default handling time can make your offers more appealing.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.480
This concludes our training on how to set one-day default handling time.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.320
Thank you and happy selling in the Amazon store.
